<h1>Is zero free for students?</h1>
<p class="answer-capsule">Yes. zero is free for students, with no premium tier, no trial window, and no fees hiding behind a feature gate. Recruiters pay for access to scored talent, so the platform earns only when your work gets you seen. You never pay to learn, submit work, get feedback, or get matched.</p>
<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>Free means free: learning, real company-shaped tasks, scoring against a professional bar, feedback, and recruiter visibility all cost students nothing.</li>
<li>The business model is the answer to "what's the catch": recruiters pay subscription fees for access to scored, matched talent. You are not the customer; your proven work is the product, and that is exactly the position you want to be in.</li>
<li>Compare the alternatives on price alone: the average coding bootcamp runs <a href="https://www.coursereport.com/coding-bootcamp-ultimate-guide">about $13,584</a>, and a degree costs many multiples of that. Both still hand you a credential and leave the proof problem unsolved.</li>
<li>Free does not mean shallow. The work is scored, the bar is professional, and weak work gets honest feedback instead of a participation badge.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>How does zero make money if students pay nothing?</h2>
<p>Recruiters and hiring teams pay for access. zero shows companies scored, verified work from students who have proven they can do the job, which saves a hiring team from wading through a thousand identical resumes and guessing. Navid, one of zero's founders, describes the ambition as "the Google ads algorithm for people": match the role to the person whose scored work fits it, and charge the company for the match.</p>
<p>Follow the money and the incentives line up in your favor. A university gets paid the day you enroll, whether or not you ever get hired. A bootcamp gets paid up front too, then points to an outcomes page. zero earns nothing until your work is good enough for a recruiter to want it, which means every part of the product is pushed toward one goal: making your work genuinely good, then making sure the right people see it.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>What do students actually get for free?</h2>
<p>The full loop, not a teaser slice of it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Real work, not videos about work.</strong> Company-shaped tasks in the role you are aiming for: the brief, the constraints, the deliverable a professional would actually produce.</li>
<li><strong>Scoring against a professional bar.</strong> Your deliverable gets evaluated the way working professionals are evaluated, with feedback specific enough to act on. Honest scores included; a low score with a clear reason is worth more than a polite certificate.</li>
<li><strong>A track record that compounds.</strong> Every scored piece of work becomes standing evidence of what you can do, the thing a transcript was never able to show.</li>
<li><strong>Recruiter visibility.</strong> Your scored work is shown to the people deciding whether to interview you, before they ever look at where you went to school.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Is there a catch, a limit, or a premium tier?</h2>
<p>No premium tier, and no plan to bolt one on, because the model does not need it. Platforms that sell to students need paywalls; platforms that sell to recruiters need good students. The honest "catch," if you want one, is effort: zero only works if you actually do the work. The scoring is real, which means mediocre effort produces a mediocre track record. Free removes the financial barrier, and it deliberately leaves the standard in place.</p>
<p>Worth saying plainly: free-with-a-real-bar is rare. Most free career products are free because the certificate is the product and brand exposure pays for it. When something is free because employers fund it, your incentives and the platform's point the same direction. The business model is the feature.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Why does the free model matter for getting hired?</h2>
<p>Because the expensive path is quietly failing the people who pay for it. <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market">Roughly 41.5% of recent graduates are underemployed</a>, working jobs that never required the degree they borrowed for. The market has been moving toward evidence of capability and away from credentials, and the products built for that old world still charge like the credential is the asset.</p>
<p>zero's bet runs the other way: make proof of capability free to build, charge the side of the market that profits from finding capable people. If the degree is a mortgage on your education, this is the version where you skip the loan and keep the asset, your own scored work.</p>
</section>

